2.1 ‘bookmark-gt-mode’
======================

‘bookmark-gt-mode’ is a global minor mode that turns on every
bookmark-gt feature described in this manual.  When ‘bookmark-gt-mode’
is off, the built-in ‘bookmark.el’ behaves as it does without
bookmark-gt.

   While ‘bookmark-gt-mode’ is on, the following features are available:

   • ‘bookmark-gt-create’ creates bookmarks.  It prompts for the name
     with a suggestion derived from the location being bookmarked (not
     from the last bookmark you jumped to), already inserted as editable
     text, so ‘RET’ accepts it and ‘C-a C-k’ replaces it.  It then
     prompts for tags, applies the same-name policy, and uses
     bookmark-gt's Dired handler when the current buffer is a Dired
     buffer.  Bookmarks whose names match ‘bookmark-gt-auto-temp-names’,
     such as ‘org-capture-last-stored’, are made temporary when they are
     stored.
   • Every jump increments the bookmark's ‘visits’ count and sets its
     ‘last-visited’ time.  This information is used to enable a
     most-recently-used ordering of the bookmarks.
   • ‘bookmark-gt-jump’ replaces ‘bookmark-jump’.  It lists the most
     recently used bookmarks first, shows marginalia annotations and
     accepts orderless narrowing particles (such as ‘;TAG’, ‘@TYPE’ and
     ‘+GROUP’) when those packages are loaded, and lets you filter by
     tag while it is reading (‘M-t’ adds a tag filter, ‘M-d’ removes the
     last one, ‘M-D’ removes all of them).
   • You can specify how a file bookmark is opened.  A file bookmark
     whose ‘filename’ matches a regexp in
     ‘bookmark-gt-file-type-handlers’ is opened by the function
     associated with that regexp instead of by ‘find-file’.
   • When a file is renamed with ‘rename-file’ (which Dired's ‘R’ uses),
     every bookmark pointing at the old name is changed to point at the
     new one.
   • Temporary bookmarks exist only for the current Emacs session.  They
     appear in the list buffer and the jump reader like any other
     bookmark, but they are never written to the bookmark file, and
     creating them never causes the file to be saved.  Making a
     temporary bookmark permanent, or a permanent one temporary, is a
     change to the bookmark file like any other.

   Turning the mode off removes every advice and hook it installed.  The
three optional modes below are turned on and off separately.


