2.2.1 ‘bookmark-gt-auto-update-mode’
------------------------------------

A bookmark with the ‘auto-update’ property is moved to the current
position of the buffer that visits its file, which is useful for keeping
your place in a book, a long document or a PDF.  The position is updated
when Emacs has been idle for ‘bookmark-gt-auto-update-interval’ seconds
(default 60), when the buffer is killed, when the window configuration
changes, and when the list buffer is opened or reverted.

   To set or clear the property, press ‘^’ in the list buffer (it acts
on the marked rows, or on the row at point when none is marked), or use
‘M-x bookmark-gt-auto-update-toggle’.  From Lisp, use
‘bookmark-gt-auto-update-set’.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: bookmark-gt-default-tags-mode,  Next: bookmark-gt-browser-tabs-mode,  Prev: bookmark-gt-auto-update-mode,  Up: Optional modes

2.2.2 ‘bookmark-gt-default-tags-mode’
-------------------------------------

This mode proposes tags for a new bookmark based on its location.  The
proposed tags become the initial input of the tag prompt, where you can
edit or clear them (see *note Tag reader: Tag reader.).  The tags come
from ‘bookmark-gt-default-tags’, which can be a single tag, a list of
tags, or a function of the newly created record that returns either:

     (setq bookmark-gt-default-tags "work")                    ; one tag
     (setq bookmark-gt-default-tags '("work" "urgent"))        ; several tags
     (setq bookmark-gt-default-tags                            ; a function
           (lambda (record)
             (cond ((string-prefix-p "/proj/" (bookmark-gt-filename-of record))
                    '("project"))
                   ((eq (bookmark-gt-handler-type record) 'url)
                    '("web"))
                   (t nil))))

   If the function signals an error, bookmark-gt shows a warning and
proposes no tags.  If ‘bookmark-gt-prompt-for-tags-flag’ is nil, there
is no tag prompt and the proposed tags are stored as they are.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: bookmark-gt-browser-tabs-mode,  Prev: bookmark-gt-default-tags-mode,  Up: Optional modes

2.2.3 ‘bookmark-gt-browser-tabs-mode’
-------------------------------------

This mode shows the tabs open in your browser as temporary URL
bookmarks.  It requires ‘browser-gt’ to be installed and connected to
the browser.

   The tab bookmarks are rebuilt each time they are about to be shown:
when ‘bookmark-gt-jump’ starts reading, when the list buffer is opened
or reverted with ‘g’, when the mode is turned on while a browser is
connected, and when you run ‘M-x bookmark-gt-browser-tabs-refresh’.
There is no timer.  A refresh never prompts for tags and never causes
the bookmark file to be saved, since tab bookmarks are temporary.

   Making a tab bookmark permanent with ‘T’ in the list buffer turns it
into an ordinary URL bookmark that is saved like any other; because the
tab is still open, the next refresh adds a temporary bookmark for it
again.  Deleting a tab bookmark deletes only the bookmark, and the next
refresh adds it again while the tab is open; as with every bookmark-gt
bookmark, deleting it does not affect what it points to.  Turning the
mode off deletes the tab bookmarks it created, and leaves alone the
bookmarks of other packages that use browser-gt.

   A tab bookmark's ‘visits’ and ‘last-visited’ count the jumps
bookmark-gt made to it, not the browser's own record of when the tab was
used, so a tab you have never jumped to is listed after those you have.
These values are kept when the tab bookmarks are rebuilt, matched by
browser name and tab id, but they are lost when the browser restarts,
when the mode is turned off, and when Emacs exits.

   Two options select which tabs are shown:
‘bookmark-gt-browser-tabs-browsers’ lists the browser clients to ask
(nil asks every connected browser), and
‘bookmark-gt-browser-tabs-filter’ is nil to keep every tab, a regexp
that the tab's URL must match, or a predicate called with the tab.


