3.1.3 Acting on several bookmarks at once
-----------------------------------------

The commands marked _selection_ in the table act on every row marked
with ‘m’, or on the row at point when no row is marked.  For example,
after marking a few rows, ‘t a’ adds the tags you type to all of them,
‘t r’ removes tags from all of them, and ‘t e’ replaces their tag lists.
The prompts say how many bookmarks are affected, and ‘t e’ and ‘t r’
offer every tag that appears on any of the marked bookmarks.

   ‘T’ and ‘^’ set the same value on every marked row: they turn the
property on for all of them, unless all of them already have it, in
which case they turn it off for all of them.  A mixed selection
therefore becomes uniform on the first press.

   A selection command clears the marks when it finishes.  If you end a
tag prompt without typing anything, nothing changes and the marks are
kept.

   Commands act only on the rows the buffer shows.  A marked row that is
hidden by a filter or by ‘H’ is left alone and keeps its mark, so you
can apply a command to part of a selection by narrowing the view first.
Tags offered by ‘t e’ and ‘t r’, and the value a toggle sets, are also
computed from the visible rows only.  If every marked row is hidden, the
command stops with "No selected bookmarks in this view" instead of
acting on the row at point.

   The deletion flag ‘D’ is separate from the selection: ‘x’ is the only
command that uses it, and the selection commands ignore it.  ‘x’ deletes
only the flagged rows the buffer shows, and refuses when all of them are
hidden.

   ‘r’, ‘R’, ‘a’, ‘i’ and the jump commands act on the row at point and
ignore the marks.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Keeping the view,  Prev: Acting on several bookmarks at once,  Up: The *Bookmarks-gt List* buffer

3.1.4 Keeping the view
----------------------

The sort column, the display of temporary bookmarks and the active
filters are kept when you call ‘bookmark-gt-list’ again in the same
session.  To keep them across Emacs sessions as well, set
‘bookmark-gt-list-persist-state’:

     (setq bookmark-gt-list-persist-state t)

   The view is then saved to ‘bookmark-gt-list-state-file’ (by default
‘(locate-user-emacs-file "bookmark-gt-list-state")’) after every change
to it and when Emacs exits, and read the next time the list buffer is
created.  Marks and the position of point are not saved.  ‘V’
(‘bookmark-gt-list-reset-view’) restores the default view.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Jumping bookmark-gt-jump,  Next: Bookmark types besides files,  Prev: The *Bookmarks-gt List* buffer,  Up: User interface

3.2 Jumping: ‘bookmark-gt-jump’
===============================

‘bookmark-gt-jump’ replaces ‘bookmark-jump’, and ‘C-x r b’ runs it while
‘bookmark-gt-mode’ is on.  It reads the bookmark with ‘completing-read’,
so it works with any completion interface, such as vertico or the
default ‘*Completions*’ buffer.  When orderless is loaded, three
particles narrow the candidates:

   • ‘@TYPE’ keeps bookmarks of TYPE (‘@URL’, ‘@Info’, ‘@PDF’, ...);
   • ‘+GROUP’ keeps bookmarks in GROUP (‘+Web’, ‘+File’, ‘+Doc’, ...);
   • ‘;TAG’ keeps bookmarks that have TAG.

   Particles are case-sensitive unless ‘completion-ignore-case’ is
non-nil, so by default you type the names as they are displayed.

   While reading, ‘M-t’ adds a tag filter and restarts the read, showing
the active filters in the prompt; ‘M-d’ removes the most recent filter,
and ‘M-D’ removes all of them.

