* The annotation::
* Using bookmark-gt from Lisp::


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: The annotation,  Next: Using bookmark-gt from Lisp,  Up: Jumping bookmark-gt-jump

3.2.1 The annotation
--------------------

With marginalia loaded, each candidate shows its tags, its type and its
location (the file name or the URL). The annotation starts at the first
multiple of 10 columns past the widest candidate name, and names are
truncated at ‘bookmark-gt-jump-name-max-width’ (default 58), so the
annotation always starts at the same column during one read.
bookmark-gt positions the annotation itself, so ‘marginalia-align’ and
‘marginalia-align-offset’ do not apply to it.

   The location takes the columns the name, tags and type leave free, so
it is wider on a wide frame.  Its width is limited by
‘bookmark-gt-jump-location-max-width’: an integer is a number of columns
(default 100), a float is a fraction of ‘marginalia-field-width’ as
marginalia computes it for the current display (at most 80), and nil
means no limit other than the free space.  The limit keeps a location
that is very long but not informative, such as a window configuration
stored in a URL, from taking every free column.  A file location is
truncated on the left so that the file name stays visible, and a URL on
the right so that the scheme and host stay visible.

   ‘marginalia-field-width’ itself does not limit these fields, because
bookmark-gt gives marginalia the widths as column counts.  As a result
the annotation can be wider than the half of the window that marginalia
normally allows.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Using bookmark-gt from Lisp,  Prev: The annotation,  Up: Jumping bookmark-gt-jump

3.2.2 Using ‘bookmark-gt’ from Lisp
-----------------------------------

‘bookmark-gt’ provides an API to create custom bookmark jumping
functions.  The three entry points take keyword arguments only:

     (cl-defun bookmark-gt-jump
         (&key bookmark display-function bookmarks-list bookmarks-filter
               sort-by preselect))

     (cl-defun bookmark-gt-jump-other-window
         (&key bookmark bookmarks-list bookmarks-filter sort-by preselect))

     (cl-defun bookmark-gt-jump-tagged
         (&key tag bookmark bookmarks-list bookmarks-filter sort-by preselect))

   • ‘:BOOKMARK’ is a bookmark name or record to jump to without
     prompting.
   • ‘:DISPLAY-FUNCTION’ is called with the target buffer, as in
     ‘bookmark-jump’.
   • ‘:BOOKMARKS-LIST’ is the alist to choose from (default
     ‘bookmark-alist’), and ‘:BOOKMARKS-FILTER’ is a predicate that a
     record must satisfy to be offered.  Both are ignored when
     ‘:BOOKMARK’ is given.
   • ‘:SORT-BY’ is ‘mru’ (most recently visited first), ‘visits’ (most
     visited first) or nil (the order of the list).  It defaults to
     ‘bookmark-gt-jump-default-sort’, which is ‘mru’.
   • ‘:PRESELECT’ is a positive integer N.  When vertico is in use, the
     reader starts with the N-th candidate selected, so with MRU sorting
     ‘:preselect 1’ selects the bookmark you visited before the last
     one.
   • ‘:TAG’ (for ‘bookmark-gt-jump-tagged’) is the tag filter the read
     starts with.

     ;; Jump to a known bookmark
     (bookmark-gt-jump :bookmark "my-bookmark")

     ;; Display the target in another frame
     (bookmark-gt-jump :display-function #'switch-to-buffer-other-frame)

     ;; Choose among file bookmarks only
     (bookmark-gt-jump :bookmarks-filter #'bookmark-gt-handler-file-p)

     ;; Choose among file bookmarks tagged "urgent"
     (bookmark-gt-jump-tagged :tag "urgent"
                              :bookmarks-filter #'bookmark-gt-handler-file-p)

   ‘bookmark-gt-jump-candidate-format-function’ (default
‘bookmark-gt-jump-candidate-default’) builds each candidate string from
a record.  A replacement must put the record in the ‘bookmark-gt-record’
text property and the name in ‘bookmark-gt-name’, and may put the
narrowing particles in ‘bookmark-gt-particles’; see the default
function's docstring.  Without ‘bookmark-gt-record’, the reader has to
find the selected bookmark by name, and cannot tell apart two bookmarks
with the same name.


