3.3.1 URL bookmarks: ‘bookmark-gt-create-url’
---------------------------------------------

‘M-x bookmark-gt-create-url’ prompts for a URL (defaulting to the URL at
point), a name and tags.  It can also be called from Lisp:

     (bookmark-gt-create-url "https://example.org" "Example" '("web" "reference"))

   Jumping opens the URL with ‘browse-url’, so it follows your
‘browse-url-browser-function’ and ‘browse-url-handlers’ settings.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Dired bookmarks,  Next: Function bookmarks bookmark-gt-create-function,  Prev: URL bookmarks bookmark-gt-create-url,  Up: Bookmark types besides files

3.3.2 Dired bookmarks
---------------------

The built-in Dired has no bookmark handler, so bookmark-gt provides
‘bookmark-gt-handler-dired-jump’.  A Dired bookmark records, and its
jump restores:

   • ‘dired-directory’, so plain directories, wildcards such as
     ‘/tmp/*.el’ and explicit file lists are all restored as they were;
   • ‘dired-marked’, the marked files and their mark characters;
   • ‘dired-subdirs’, the subdirectories inserted with ‘i’;
   • ‘dired-hidden-dirs’, the subdirectories hidden with ‘$’;
   • ‘dired-switches’, the ‘ls’ switches in effect.

   These keys are the ones bookmark+ uses (‘bmkp-jump-dired’), so Dired
bookmarks can be moved between the two packages in either direction.
Records with bookmark+'s handler are classified as Dired, and ‘M-x
bookmark-gt-migrate-from-bookmark-plus’ converts them to bookmark-gt's
handler (see *note Migrating from bookmark+::).

  1. Virtual Dired

     A bookmark created in a virtual Dired buffer (‘M-x dired-virtual’,
     or ‘V’ in ‘dired-x’) also stores the buffer's listing under
     ‘dired-listing’ and sets ‘dired-virtual’.  Jumping recreates the
     same buffer from the stored listing, even if the directory no
     longer exists.  bookmark+ does not do this; it opens the directory
     as an ordinary Dired buffer.

     Because the bookmark file is read whole at startup and written
     whole on every save, the listing is limited to
     ‘bookmark-gt-dired-virtual-max-size’ bytes (default 524288, that is
     512 KB). Creating a bookmark in a larger virtual Dired buffer is an
     error.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Function bookmarks bookmark-gt-create-function,  Next: Keyboard-macro bookmarks bookmark-gt-create-kmacro,  Prev: Dired bookmarks,  Up: Bookmark types besides files

3.3.3 Function bookmarks: ‘bookmark-gt-create-function’
-------------------------------------------------------

A function bookmark stores a function (a symbol or a lambda) under the
‘function’ key, and jumping calls it.  ‘M-x bookmark-gt-create-function’
prompts for the name and the command.  From Lisp:

     (bookmark-gt-create-function "rebuild" #'my-project-rebuild)
     (bookmark-gt-create-function "flip-theme" (lambda () (my-toggle-theme)))


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Keyboard-macro bookmarks bookmark-gt-create-kmacro,  Next: Sequence bookmarks bookmark-gt-create-sequence,  Prev: Function bookmarks bookmark-gt-create-function,  Up: Bookmark types besides files

3.3.4 Keyboard-macro bookmarks: ‘bookmark-gt-create-kmacro’
-----------------------------------------------------------

A keyboard-macro bookmark stores a vector of key events under the
‘kmacro’ key, and jumping replays it with ‘execute-kbd-macro’, using the
prefix argument as the repeat count.

   ‘M-x bookmark-gt-create-kmacro’ offers your named keyboard macros,
plus ‘[last-kbd-macro]’ for the most recently defined one, which is the
default when it exists; it is an error if there is no macro at all.  A
named macro is a symbol whose function definition is a string or vector,
or that has a ‘kmacro’ property, as created by ‘M-x
kmacro-name-last-macro’.  From Lisp:

     (bookmark-gt-create-kmacro "type-hi" [?h ?i])
     (bookmark-gt-create-kmacro "type-abc" "abc")   ; parsed by `read-kbd-macro'

   bookmark+ stores keyboard macros as function bookmarks.  bookmark-gt
gives them a type of their own so that the list buffer and the jump
reader can distinguish them, which means a bookmark+ macro bookmark is
shown as a Function bookmark in bookmark-gt.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Sequence bookmarks bookmark-gt-create-sequence,  Next: File-type handlers,  Prev: Keyboard-macro bookmarks bookmark-gt-create-kmacro,  Up: Bookmark types besides files

3.3.5 Sequence bookmarks: ‘bookmark-gt-create-sequence’
-------------------------------------------------------

A sequence bookmark jumps to a list of bookmarks in order.  ‘M-x
bookmark-gt-create-sequence’ prompts for its name and then for existing
bookmarks, one per prompt, until you enter an empty input.  From Lisp:

     (bookmark-gt-create-sequence "morning-workspace"
                                  '("inbox" "todo.org" "calendar"))

   The members are stored under the ‘sequence’ key.  A member name that
matches exactly one bookmark is replaced by that bookmark's id (see
*note Record identity::), so the sequence still works after the member
is renamed.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: File-type handlers,  Next: View bookmarks,  Prev: Sequence bookmarks bookmark-gt-create-sequence,  Up: Bookmark types besides files

3.3.6 File-type handlers
------------------------

‘bookmark-gt’ provides a way to specify how to open bookmarks.
‘bookmark-gt-file-type-handlers’ maps file-name regexps to bookmark
handler functions.  Jumping to a file bookmark whose ‘filename’ matches
one of the regexps calls the associated function with the record,
instead of visiting the file.  The first matching entry is used.  For
example, to open PDF and video files with macOS's ‘open’ command:

     (defun my-mac-open (bookmark)
       "Open BOOKMARK's file with macOS's `open' command."
       (call-process "open" nil 0 nil
                     (bookmark-prop-get bookmark 'filename)))

     (setq bookmark-gt-file-type-handlers
           '(("\\.pdf\\'"               . my-mac-open)
             ("\\.\\(mp4\\|mkv\\)\\'"   . my-mac-open)))


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: View bookmarks,  Prev: File-type handlers,  Up: Bookmark types besides files

3.3.7 View bookmarks
--------------------

It is possible to create a bookmark to the current state of the
‘*Bookmarks-gt List*’ buffer.

   A view bookmark stores the list buffer's filters, sort column and
display of temporary bookmarks (under the ‘filters’, ‘sort-key’ and
‘show-temp’ keys).  To create one, set up the list buffer the way you
want it and run ‘M-x bookmark-gt-create’ there.  Jumping to it opens the
list buffer with that view.  Marks and the position of point are not
stored.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Creating and changing bookmarks,  Next: Bookmarks in the buffer,  Prev: User interface,  Up: Top

4 Creating and changing bookmarks
*********************************

* Menu:

* Tag reader::
* Bookmarks that share a name::
* Changing an existing bookmark::
