File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Tag reader,  Next: Bookmarks that share a name,  Up: Creating and changing bookmarks

4.1 Tag reader
==============

‘bookmark-gt-tags-read’ asks for tags one at a time, completing over the
tags already in use, most recently used first.  Each non-empty ‘RET’
adds a tag and asks for the next; an empty ‘RET’ finishes, and ‘M-RET’
adds the current input and finishes.  The prompt shows the tags entered
so far, as in ‘Tags [foo,bar] (M-RET or empty RET to finish):’.

   The creation commands (‘bookmark-gt-create’,
‘bookmark-gt-create-url’, ‘bookmark-gt-create-function’,
‘bookmark-gt-create-kmacro’ and ‘bookmark-gt-create-sequence’) compute
the tags of a new bookmark in three steps:

  1. if ‘bookmark-gt-default-tags-mode’ is on, the default tags;
  2. if ‘bookmark-gt-prompt-for-tags-flag’ is non-nil (the default), the
     tag reader, starting with the tags from step 1;
  3. the functions on ‘bookmark-gt-create-tag-reader-hook’, which
     receive the tags from step 2.

   These steps run after the name has been accepted, so a name that
‘bookmark-gt-allow-same-name-bookmarks’ refuses is reported before you
are asked for tags.

   From Lisp, the TAGS argument of a creation command is either a list
of tags, which is stored as given with no prompt, or ‘t’, which runs the
three steps (interactive calls pass ‘t’):

     (bookmark-gt-create-url "https://example.org" "example" '("web"))  ; stores ("web")
     (bookmark-gt-create-url "https://example.org" "example" t)         ; runs the steps

   ‘bookmark-gt-create-non-file’, which creates a record with a given
handler, never runs these steps; the record gets the ‘tags’ entry of its
PROPS argument, if any.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Bookmarks that share a name,  Next: Changing an existing bookmark,  Prev: Tag reader,  Up: Creating and changing bookmarks

4.2 Bookmarks that share a name
===============================

A bookmark name is a label, not an identifier, so several bookmarks may
share one, such as a ‘todo’ bookmark in each of several projects.  Two
bookmarks with the same name and the same destination, however, are
almost always a mistake.  ‘bookmark-gt-allow-same-name-bookmarks’
decides whether ‘bookmark-gt-create’ may reuse a name:

Value                               Creating with a name already in use
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘never’                             refused
‘different-destination’ (default)   allowed if the two point at different files or URLs, refused if at the same
‘always’                            allowed

   The destination is the file or URL, not the position, so two
bookmarks at different places in one file have the same destination.  A
refused name is an error that names the conflicting bookmark; the new
bookmark is not stored under a modified name.  If you meant to move the
existing bookmark, use ‘bookmark-gt-update’, as the error message
suggests.

   The names in ‘bookmark-gt-unique-names’ (by default those of
‘org-capture’ and ‘org-refile’) always identify a single bookmark,
whatever the setting.  For handlers in
‘bookmark-gt-unique-name-handlers’, storing a bookmark with the name of
an existing bookmark of the same handler replaces it.

* Menu:

* Telling them apart::
* Loading a file that breaks the setting::
* When you expect two bookmarks and see one::
