File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Telling them apart,  Next: Loading a file that breaks the setting,  Up: Bookmarks that share a name

4.2.1 Telling them apart
------------------------

Bookmarks that share a name are displayed with a suffix, as in ‘todo’
and ‘todo<2>’, the way Emacs names buffers.  The suffix is only
displayed: the bookmark file contains the name you typed, so deleting or
renaming one of the bookmarks renumbers the others.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Loading a file that breaks the setting,  Next: When you expect two bookmarks and see one,  Prev: Telling them apart,  Up: Bookmarks that share a name

4.2.2 Loading a file that breaks the setting
--------------------------------------------

A bookmark file can contain names the setting forbids, if it was written
by the built-in bookmark package, by bookmark-gt with a different
setting, or on another machine.  When such a file is loaded, the
bookmarks the setting forbids are left out of ‘bookmark-alist’, and a
warning shows each of them in full, since until the file is saved the
warning is the only other copy.  The first bookmark with a given name is
always kept, and under the default setting a later one is kept if it
points somewhere no kept bookmark does.  The same check runs when the
list buffer is opened or reverted and when the jump reader starts, so it
also catches bookmarks created some other way, such as by ‘bookmark-set’
called from Lisp.

   The bookmarks left out are still in the file, and they are removed
from it the next time it is saved.  To keep them, set the option to
‘always’ and load the file again before saving.  Leaving them out does
not by itself cause the file to be saved.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: When you expect two bookmarks and see one,  Next: Which command acts on which bookmark,  Prev: Loading a file that breaks the setting,  Up: Bookmarks that share a name

4.2.3 When you expect two bookmarks and see one
-----------------------------------------------

First count the bookmarks with that name:

     (seq-filter (lambda (r)
                   (equal (substring-no-properties (car r)) "todo"))
                 bookmark-alist)

   If there are two, the list buffer is not showing one of them, either
because a filter is active (filters can be kept from an earlier session;
‘V’ removes them), or because one of them is temporary and temporary
bookmarks are hidden (‘H’ shows them; ‘T’ is different, it makes the
bookmark at point temporary or permanent).

   If there is one, the second was never stored: either
‘bookmark-gt-create’ refused it, with an error at the time, or it was
left out when the file was loaded, with a warning listing it (see the
previous section).


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Which command acts on which bookmark,  Next: Record identity,  Prev: When you expect two bookmarks and see one,  Up: Bookmarks that share a name

4.2.4 Which command acts on which bookmark
------------------------------------------

The built-in ‘bookmark-get-bookmark’ looks a name up with ‘assoc’, so
the built-in commands act on the first bookmark with that name.
bookmark-gt's commands work with records: given a record, they act on
it, and given a name that several bookmarks share, they ask which one.
Selecting the second of two ‘todo’ bookmarks in the jump reader or the
list buffer therefore jumps to, renames, relocates or deletes that
bookmark, and the jump counts as a visit to it.

   While ‘bookmark-gt-mode’ is on, ‘M-x bookmark-delete’,
‘bookmark-rename’, ‘bookmark-jump’ and ‘bookmark-set’ run bookmark-gt's
versions.  The functions themselves are not changed, because packages
depend on their behavior: ‘org-capture’ and ‘org-refile’, for example,
store a bookmark under a fixed name each time and rely on ‘bookmark-set’
replacing the previous one.  From Lisp, ‘bookmark-gt-jump-record’,
‘bookmark-gt-rename-record’ and ‘bookmark-gt-delete-record’ take a
record, and signal an error when given a name.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Record identity,  Prev: Which command acts on which bookmark,  Up: Bookmarks that share a name

4.2.5 Record identity
---------------------

Each bookmark has a ‘bookmark-gt-id’, a symbol assigned when the
bookmark is created; bookmarks that lack one (for example, because
another package wrote them) get one when the bookmark file is loaded,
the list buffer is drawn or the jump reader starts.  The id lets a
reference outlive a rename, which is why sequence bookmarks store their
members by id.  You never type an id; it appears only in ‘i’ in the list
buffer and in the bookmark file, and the built-in bookmark package
ignores it.  A file copied between machines can contain the same id
twice; a bookmark is looked up by its id when it has one, and by its
name otherwise.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Changing an existing bookmark,  Next: Deleting,  Prev: Bookmarks that share a name,  Up: Creating and changing bookmarks

4.3 Changing an existing bookmark
=================================

Command                  Changes                                      Run it from
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘bookmark-gt-update’     where it points: to point                    the new location
‘bookmark-gt-relocate’   where it points: to a file or URL you type   anywhere
‘bookmark-gt-rename’     its name                                     anywhere

   Each command takes a bookmark record, name or id, and prompts when
given none.  Given a name that several bookmarks share, it asks which
one.

* Menu:

* bookmark-gt-update::
* bookmark-gt-relocate::
* bookmark-gt-rename::


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: bookmark-gt-update,  Next: bookmark-gt-relocate,  Up: Changing an existing bookmark

4.3.1 ‘bookmark-gt-update’
--------------------------

‘bookmark-gt-update’ records the current location and stores it in the
existing bookmark.  The bookmark keeps its name, id, tags, annotation,
creation time and visit history; everything that describes where it
points is replaced, so data left from its previous type (a PDF page, an
Org heading id, Dired marks) is removed.  It asks for confirmation
before changing the bookmark's type, for example from PDF to Org.  It
cannot update URL, function, keyboard-macro or sequence bookmarks, since
they have no location in a buffer; use ‘bookmark-gt-relocate’ for those.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: bookmark-gt-relocate,  Next: bookmark-gt-rename,  Prev: bookmark-gt-update,  Up: Changing an existing bookmark

4.3.2 ‘bookmark-gt-relocate’
----------------------------

‘bookmark-gt-relocate’ asks for the new target instead of recording the
current location, so it works without visiting the target.  For a file
bookmark it reads a file name, starting from the current one, and keeps
the position, since the usual reason to relocate is a file that was
moved.  For a URL bookmark, including a browser-tab bookmark, it reads a
URL.  Other bookmarks cannot be relocated.  It refuses a target of a
different type (an Org file for a PDF bookmark, for example), because
the bookmark's handler could not open it; use ‘bookmark-gt-update’ to
change the type.  In the list buffer, ‘R’ relocates the bookmark at
point.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: bookmark-gt-rename,  Prev: bookmark-gt-relocate,  Up: Changing an existing bookmark

4.3.3 ‘bookmark-gt-rename’
--------------------------

‘bookmark-gt-rename’ renames the bookmark you select, not the first one
with its name.  The new name is checked the same way as a new bookmark's
name (see *note Bookmarks that share a name::).  In the list buffer, ‘r’
renames the bookmark at point.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Deleting,  Prev: Changing an existing bookmark,  Up: Creating and changing bookmarks

4.4 Deleting
============

‘M-x bookmark-gt-delete’ deletes the bookmark you select, not the first
one with its name.  If a sequence bookmark refers to it, the command
tells you before deleting, since that reference will no longer work.  In
the list buffer, ‘d’ flags rows and ‘x’ deletes them.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Bookmarks in the buffer,  Next: Tracking,  Prev: Creating and changing bookmarks,  Up: Top

5 Bookmarks in the buffer
*************************

* Menu:

* Cycling through a file's bookmarks::
