5.1 Cycling through a file's bookmarks
======================================

‘M-x bookmark-gt-cycle-next’ and ‘M-x bookmark-gt-cycle-prev’ move point
to the next or previous bookmark in the current file, ordered by
position, wrapping around at either end and showing the bookmark's name.
Bookmarks without a numeric position, such as Dired bookmarks, are
skipped.  Cycling does not depend on highlighting being on.  Example
bindings:

     (global-set-key (kbd "C-c n") #'bookmark-gt-cycle-next)
     (global-set-key (kbd "C-c p") #'bookmark-gt-cycle-prev)


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Highlighting,  Next: Region bookmarks,  Prev: Cycling through a file's bookmarks,  Up: Bookmarks in the buffer

5.2 Highlighting
================

While ‘bookmark-gt-mode’ is on, each bookmark is highlighted in the
buffers that visit its file, using ‘bookmark-gt-face-highlight’ (which
inherits from ‘hl-line’).  A bookmark at a point highlights its line,
and a region bookmark highlights the lines of its region; moving the
mouse over the highlight shows the bookmark's name.

   Highlights are added when a file is visited and updated after each
change to a bookmark.  A bookmark without a numeric position, such as an
Org heading bookmark, is highlighted at the place its last jump arrived,
once you have jumped to it.  Highlights are not saved, and they are
removed when the mode is turned off.  To turn them off while keeping the
mode on, set ‘bookmark-gt-highlight-enable’ to nil.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Region bookmarks,  Prev: Highlighting,  Up: Bookmarks in the buffer

5.3 Region bookmarks
====================

If the region is active when you run ‘bookmark-gt-create’, the bookmark
stores the region and selects it again after a jump.  The record stores
the start in ‘position’ and the end in ‘end-position’, plus
‘bookmark-gt-region-context-size’ characters (default 40) before and
after the end, under ‘front-context-region-string’ and
‘rear-context-region-string’, which locate the end again if the text has
changed.  These keys are the ones bookmark+ uses; the built-in bookmark
package ignores them and jumps to ‘position’.  To neither store nor
restore regions, set ‘bookmark-gt-use-region’ to nil.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Tracking,  Next: Extending bookmark-gt,  Prev: Bookmarks in the buffer,  Up: Top

6 Tracking
**********

* Menu:

* Visits::
* File renames::


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Visits,  Next: File renames,  Up: Tracking

6.1 Visits
==========

Every jump increments the bookmark's ‘visits’ count and sets its
‘last-visited’ time, which is what the jump reader's ‘mru’ and ‘visits’
sorting use.  These changes do not mark the bookmark file as modified,
so jumping never causes a save; they are written the next time the file
is saved for another reason.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: File renames,  Prev: Visits,  Up: Tracking

6.2 File renames
================

While ‘bookmark-gt-mode’ is on, renaming a file with ‘rename-file’,
which Dired's ‘R’ also uses, changes every bookmark that points at the
old file name to point at the new one.  Renaming a directory changes
only bookmarks that point at the directory itself, not at files inside
it.  Renames to a backup file name are ignored, because Emacs renames a
file to its backup name when it saves it, and the bookmark should stay
with the file.  To turn rename tracking off, set
‘bookmark-gt-track-renames’ to nil.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Extending bookmark-gt,  Next: Compatibility,  Prev: Tracking,  Up: Top

7 Extending bookmark-gt
***********************

* Menu:

* Handler registry::
* Group registry::
* Hooks and registries::


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Handler registry,  Next: Group registry,  Up: Extending bookmark-gt

7.1 Handler registry
====================

‘bookmark-gt-handler-alist’ maps each handler function to a description
of its bookmark type:

     (HANDLER . (:type TYPE-SYMBOL :name STRING :group GROUP-SYMBOL
                 :face FACE :doc STRING))

   Several handlers can share a type; URL bookmarks, for example, have
three handler names.  bookmark-gt registers file, URL, EWW, Dired, Info,
Org, Org-roam (org-roam-gt), PDF, browser tab, Magit, Help, Image and
EPUB (nov.el) bookmarks.  To register a new handler, or another name for
an existing type:

     (bookmark-gt-handler-register
      '(my-custom-jump my-legacy-jump-handler)
      (list :type 'custom :name "Custom" :group 'other
            :face 'bookmark-gt-face-file
            :doc "My custom bookmark type."
            :preview #'my-preview-fn))   ; optional

   The optional ‘:preview’ function is what ‘TAB’ in the list buffer
calls with the record, inside ‘save-selected-window’; without it, ‘TAB’
jumps to the bookmark in another window.  A preview function lets you,
for example, show a URL in EWW while ‘RET’ still opens it in the
external browser.

   A handler that is not registered is classified automatically.  If it
has a ‘bookmark-handler-type’ property, as ‘bookmark-bmenu-list’
expects, that is its type name.  Otherwise its name is used without the
usual suffixes (‘-bookmark-jump-handler’, ‘-bookmark-jump’,
‘-jump-handler’, ‘--handle-bookmark’), so ‘foo-bookmark-jump’ is shown
as type "Foo".  Either way, its group is ‘other’.

   Each registered type has a predicate: ‘bookmark-gt-handler-file-p’,
‘-url-p’, ‘-eww-p’, ‘-dired-p’, ‘-info-p’, ‘-org-p’, ‘-org-roam-p’,
‘-pdf-p’, ‘-browser-tab-p’, ‘-magit-p’, ‘-help-p’, ‘-image-p’,
‘-epub-p’, ‘-function-p’, ‘-sequence-p’, ‘-kmacro-p’ and ‘-view-p’, each
equivalent to ‘(eq (bookmark-gt-handler-type record) 'TYPE)’.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Group registry,  Next: Hooks and registries,  Prev: Handler registry,  Up: Extending bookmark-gt

7.2 Group registry
==================

Groups combine several types into one category, so that you can filter
by "web" instead of by URL, EWW and browser tab separately.  A
bookmark's group is computed from its handler's registration and is not
stored in the record.

Symbol    Name    Types
---------------------------------------------------------
‘web’     Web     url, eww, browser-tab
‘file’    File    file, dired
‘doc’     Doc     info, org, org-roam, pdf, help, epub
‘code’    Code    magit
‘media’   Media   image
‘other’   Other   everything else

   ‘bookmark-gt-group-alist’ holds the groups.  To add one:

     (bookmark-gt-group-register
      'science '(:name "Sci" :doc "Science and research bookmarks."))

   The group is shown in the list buffer's Group column, can be filtered
with ‘/’, and is matched by the ‘+GROUP’ particle in the jump reader.
‘(bookmark-gt-handler-group RECORD)’ returns a record's group.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Hooks and registries,  Prev: Group registry,  Up: Extending bookmark-gt

7.3 Hooks and registries
========================

   • ‘bookmark-gt-create-name-reader-hook’ runs in ‘bookmark-gt-create’
     before the name prompt.  Each function is called with the suggested
     name and the new record's data, and can return a different
     suggestion; the first non-nil value is used.
   • ‘bookmark-gt-create-tag-reader-hook’ is the last step of the tag
     computation (see *note Tag reader: Tag reader.); it does not run in
     ‘bookmark-gt-create-non-file’.  Each function is called with the
     record and the tags so far, and returns the tags passed to the next
     function.
   • ‘bookmark-gt-record-changed-hook’ runs after every change to a
     bookmark, with the record and a symbol naming the change: ‘create’,
     ‘update’, ‘relocate’, ‘rename’, ‘tags’, ‘temp’, ‘auto-update’ or
     ‘delete’.
   • ‘bookmark-gt-jump-before-read-hook’ runs once each time
     ‘bookmark-gt-jump’, ‘bookmark-gt-jump-other-window’ or
     ‘bookmark-gt-jump-tagged’ is about to prompt (not when ‘:BOOKMARK’
     is given, and not again when a tag filter restarts the read).  Use
     it to rebuild bookmarks that must be current when the reader shows
     them; ‘bookmark-gt-browser-tabs-mode’ adds
     ‘bookmark-gt-browser-tabs-refresh’ to it.
   • ‘bookmark-gt-list-before-read-hook’ runs when the list buffer is
     opened or reverted, before the rows are drawn.
     ‘bookmark-gt-browser-tabs-mode’ adds
     ‘bookmark-gt-browser-tabs-refresh’ to it, and
     ‘bookmark-gt-auto-update-mode’ adds ‘bookmark-gt-auto-update-tick’.
   • ‘bookmark-gt-session-only-props’ lists record keys whose values are
     valid only while another program is running, such as a browser tab
     id.  When a temporary bookmark is made permanent, these keys are
     removed from it, so they are never saved.
     ‘bookmark-gt-browser-tabs-mode’ registers the three keys its tab
     bookmarks use.
   • ‘bookmark-gt-filter-alist’ and ‘bookmark-gt-sort-alist’ hold the
     list buffer's filters (for ‘/’) and sort orders.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Compatibility,  Next: Reference,  Prev: Extending bookmark-gt,  Up: Top

8 Compatibility
***************

* Menu:

* Data compatibility with bookmark+::
* Migrating from bookmark+::


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Data compatibility with bookmark+,  Next: Migrating from bookmark+,  Up: Compatibility

8.1 Data compatibility with bookmark+
=====================================

bookmark-gt reads and writes these record keys with the same meaning as
bookmark+, so either package can read the other's bookmark file:

Key               Meaning
----------------------------------------------------
‘tags’            List of tag strings
‘bmkp-temp’       The bookmark is temporary
‘auto-update’     The position is kept up to date
‘visits’          Number of visits
‘last-visited’    Time of the last visit
‘created’         Time of creation
‘last-modified’   Time of the last change

   Two differences are handled when reading.  bookmark+ stores the
string ‘" - no file -"’ in ‘filename’ for bookmarks without a file,
which ‘bookmark-gt-filename-of’ treats as no file name; bookmark-gt
omits ‘filename’ for such bookmarks.  bookmark+ stores a URL under
‘location’, while bookmark-gt uses ‘url’; ‘bookmark-gt-url-of’ reads
both.  bookmark-gt adds one key of its own, ‘bookmark-gt-id’ (see *note
Record identity::), which bookmark+ and the built-in bookmark package
ignore.


File: bookmark-gt.info,  Node: Migrating from bookmark+,  Prev: Data compatibility with bookmark+,  Up: Compatibility

8.2 Migrating from bookmark+
============================

Bookmarks saved by bookmark+ name handler functions that exist only when
bookmark+ is loaded (‘bmkp-jump-dired’, ‘bmkp-jump-url-browse’,
‘bmkp-jump-url-browse-other-window’ and ‘bmkp-gt-browsel-tabs-jump’).
bookmark-gt classifies them correctly in the list buffer and the jump
reader, but jumping to them fails because the function is not defined.
‘M-x bookmark-gt-migrate-from-bookmark-plus’ converts them once:

     ;; Changes `bookmark-alist' only; the file is not written.
     (bookmark-gt-migrate-from-bookmark-plus)
     ;; Check the result in M-x bookmark-gt-list, then save:
     (bookmark-save)

   For each bookmark+ record, the migration replaces the handler with
bookmark-gt's, moves a URL from ‘location’ to ‘url’, and removes the ‘"
- no file -"’ placeholder from ‘filename’.  It also removes keys that
bookmark-gt does not use: ‘bmkp-gt-load-index’, ‘buffer-name’,
‘front-context-region-string’, ‘rear-context-region-string’, and an
empty ‘annotation’ or ‘tags’.  It keeps a real ‘filename’, ‘position’,
‘front-context-string’, ‘rear-context-string’, ‘visits’, ‘last-visited’,
‘created’, ‘last-modified’ and the Dired keys, which bookmark-gt's Dired
handler reads the same way bookmark+'s did.  Records already in
bookmark-gt's form, and plain file bookmarks, are not changed.  Keep a
copy of your bookmark file before migrating.


